.TH CCOL_CIRCQ_MSG_COUNT 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_circq_msg_count \- get the current message count of a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

size_t ccol_circq_msg_count(ccol_circular_queue *cq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function gives the number of messages that the queue holds now. It
reads that number under the lock of the queue. The value is therefore a
consistent snapshot and not a torn read.

The value is only a snapshot. Another thread can send or receive the
instant that the function unlocks the queue. The value therefore describes
a moment that already passed when the function returns it. Use the value
for reports and for a drain loop. To learn whether a queue has room or
holds a message, use ccol_circq_try_send_zc() or ccol_circq_try_recv_zc().
Each of those calls gives the answer and does the action in one step that
nothing can break apart.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq   Circular queue to ask
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The function gives the number of messages that the queue holds now.
It gives ccol_invalid_size ((size_t)-1) when cq is NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The count is a thread-safe snapshot.
Zero is an ordinary result, and it means that the queue is empty. Only
ccol_invalid_size shows an error.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circq_try_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_disable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_chan_msg_count (3)
